  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will slide through the area this morning, with\nshowers ending early on. At least partial clearing takes place\ndownwind of the mountains, with the mountain zones remaining\ncloudy. Lower elevations warm to the 60s, with colder and windy\nconditions in the mountains. Elsewhere, wind gusts of 15 to 25\nMPH are expected.\n\nCooler, drier, and not as breezy into Thursday as temp values \nfall behind the cold front, but manage to rise into the 50s to\naround 60 outside of the mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure will be strengthening through the Great Lakes\nThursday night as a sharp mid level trough digs through\nMichigan. Regional jet dynamics will be supportive of a low \nthat continues to strengthen through Saturday morning into the \nCanadian Maritimes.\n\nThis should bring another round of beneficial rain to the area,\nin addition to blustery conditions behind (see KM3). Rain should\nbe underway Friday morning for NH, increasing east into western\nME by noon. The storm is progressive, so rain won't linger much\nmore than 8 to 12 hours in one location. Winds quickly shift NW\nFriday evening, and this will begin a period of CAA and upslope\nrain/snow showers for the mountains lasting through Saturday. \n\nNBM QPF continues to trend greater for this event, but values\nremain below that of today's system...around three quarters of\nan inch. Wintry precip will again be possible with this event,\nwith the first round bound to higher elevations Friday morning.\nThe second round, with more confidence, will be associated with\nthe cooler NW winds arriving Friday night into Saturday. These\nsnow showers could fall more widespread across northern NH and \nwestern ME, mainly in the mountains but perhaps some snow \nshowers spill into the foothills given the broad 35 to 40 kt low\nlevel wind field.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nForecast confidence is above average for a blustery weekend \nwith well below normal temperatures for mid-October. A strong \nsurface cold front will push of the coast early Saturday \nmorning, with strong NW flow in its wake as a mid level trough \naxis swings overhead. Anomalous NW flow from 850mb to the \nsurface will support a gusty day with the potential for power \noutage impacts, especially in the eastern lee-sides of the \nmountains in downslope favored areas. Will have to watch this \ntrend through the week, but gusts 40+ mph are possible. A\nwind advisory may be needed Saturday. In addition to the winds,\nit will be cold with wind chill temperatures making it feel \nmore like winter than fall. Higher elevations will experience \nfull on winter conditions and will be dangerous above the tree \nline, fall hiking is not recommended. Cold weather will continue\nthrough the weekend with sub-freezing temperatures possible \nSaturday and Sunday night.",
